A7 — Audio Quality Complaints

⸻

1️⃣ Problem Clarification

Users report distorted, corrupted, silent, or glitchy voice messages.

This is not an “AI audio enhancement” problem.

It is a reliability monitoring and diagnosis problem.

What we are solving
	•	Detect technically degraded voice messages
	•	Identify patterns across device / app version / codec / network
	•	Help engineering reduce failure rates
	•	Prevent regressions after releases

Who this system is for
	•	Engineering (client, codec, pipeline)
	•	QA / Release managers
	•	Product leadership monitoring reliability

What decision this informs
	•	Should we roll back a release?
	•	Which cohort (device × version × codec × network) is risky?
	•	Where should engineering focus?
	•	Should users be prompted to re-record?

Plain-English Problem Statement

Build a system that detects technically degraded voice messages using signal characteristics and metadata, without pre-existing labels, and surfaces actionable insights to reduce failure rates and prevent regressions.

This is a signal integrity monitoring system, not a speech understanding system.

⸻

2️⃣ Problem Type Classification

Because there are no labeled examples of “bad audio”, this is not initially a supervised learning problem.

Correct framing (phase-based)
	•	Unsupervised anomaly detection
	•	Cohort-level statistical analysis
	•	Optional later: Weak supervision → supervised model

This aligns with the ML Workflow principles in the Master AI folder:
	•	Define the real goal first
	•	Start simple
	•	Avoid modeling without labels
	•	Build structure before prediction

⸻

3️⃣ Data Science Scope

Available Data

Audio
	•	Raw waveform or encoded file
	•	Duration
	•	Bitrate
	•	Sample rate

Metadata
	•	Device model
	•	OS version
	•	App version
	•	Codec type
	•	Network type
	•	Upload retries
	•	Transcoder version
	•	Region
	•	Client error logs

This is a multi-modal dataset:
	•	Audio signal
	•	Structured metadata
	•	Time-series (release-level drift)

⸻

Granularity
	•	Message-level (primary)
	•	Cohort-level (device × version × codec)
	•	Release-level (time component)

⸻

Labels

None initially.

Possible creation paths:
	•	Human QA review of anomaly samples
	•	Internal structured scoring (clipping, dropout, silence, etc.)
	•	Weak behavioral signals (re-record, complaint taps)

⸻

Explicit Data Risks
	•	“Bad” is multi-class, not binary
	•	Complaint data is biased
	•	Privacy implications (voice content)
	•	Device imbalance
	•	Risk of leakage if complaint behavior becomes proxy label

⸻

4️⃣ Modeling Approach (Incremental and Realistic)

Phase 0 — Non-ML First

Before ML:
	•	Define failure taxonomy:
	•	Clipping
	•	Dropouts
	•	Excessive silence
	•	Codec artifacts
	•	Sample rate mismatch
	•	Implement integrity checks:
	•	Duration mismatch
	•	Frame discontinuity
	•	Silence thresholds
	•	Build release-level dashboards

This follows the ML Workflow discipline: start with the baseline.

⸻

Phase 1 — Feature Engineering

Extract signal health features:
	•	RMS energy (mean / variance)
	•	% clipped samples
	•	Silence ratio
	•	Spectral centroid
	•	Spectral flatness
	•	Frame discontinuity count
	•	File-size vs duration anomaly

Join with metadata.

This mirrors prior Feature Engineering and EDA work in the Master AI folder:
Understand structure before modeling.

⸻

Phase 2 — Unsupervised Detection

Use:
	•	Isolation Forest
	•	One-Class SVM
	•	Autoencoder reconstruction error on spectrogram features

Goal:
	•	Rank suspicious messages
	•	Surface high-risk cohorts
	•	Detect systemic shifts

This connects directly to:
	•	Outlier detection concepts
	•	Bias–variance reasoning
	•	Monitoring for drift

⸻

Phase 3 — Weak Supervision (Optional)

Create heuristic labeling functions:
	•	If clipping > threshold → likely distorted
	•	If silence ratio > threshold → blank
	•	If duration mismatch → corrupted

Combine multiple noisy rules to generate probabilistic labels.

Then optionally train a supervised classifier once labels are validated.

⸻

Explicit Non-Goals
	•	No speech-to-text
	•	No NLP modeling
	•	No end-to-end deep audio models initially
	•	No content-level judgment

This is about system integrity, not semantic content.

⸻

5️⃣ Use of LLMs (Critical Reflection)

How LLMs were used
	•	Enumerating technical failure modes
	•	Exploring anomaly detection strategies
	•	Identifying weak supervision as a label bootstrapping method

Where I corrected the LLM tendency

LLMs often default to:
	•	Deep end-to-end models
	•	Immediate classification pipelines

I constrained the scope to:
	•	Signal integrity features
	•	Diagnosable engineering outputs
	•	Incremental modeling

This keeps the project grounded in product impact, not modeling novelty.

⸻

6️⃣ Unknown Methods Discovery

Weak Supervision

Concept:

Instead of manually labeling thousands of samples, define multiple imperfect heuristic rules. Each rule is noisy. A probabilistic model learns how reliable each rule is and generates soft labels.

Initial incorrect assumption:

No labels = no training.

Correction:

You can bootstrap labels using structured heuristics and anomaly detection.

